{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This session is being continued from a previous conversation that ran out of context. The summary below covers the earlier portion of the conversation.\n\nSummary:\n1. Primary Request and Intent:\n   The user submitted a cobbler-scaffold orchestrator task (Task ID: 5596, \"[measuring] generation-run44 task 0\") for the go-unix-utils project. This is a code generation task within an automated pipeline that generates Go reimplementations of GNU coreutils/moreutils utilities. The task involves implementing utilities based on requirements marked as \"ready\" in the requirement_states. The requirement_states show ready requirements for SRDs: srd004-ts, srd103-pathchk, srd104-test, srd105-stty, srd106-df, srd107-dir, srd108-vdir, srd109-dircolors, srd110-pr, srd111-ptx, srd112-chronic, srd113-pee, srd114-vidir. The task instructions say to review context, plan approach, implement, and verify.\n\n2. Key Technical Concepts:\n   - Go reimplementations of GNU coreutils verified via differential testing against Homebrew reference binaries\n   - Differential testing pattern: `testutils.RunDiffTests(t, goBin, refBin, tests)` comparing stdout, stderr, exit code\n   - SIGPIPE handling: all cmd/ utilities must call `sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler()` at start of main\n   - Graceful skip: tests must `exec.LookPath` for reference binary and `t.Skip` if not found\n   - `LC_ALL=C` set by default in test harness\n   - Configuration: `max_requirements_per_task: 4`\n   - Execution constitution: E1-E10 articles (spec-first, traceability, no scope creep, no git commands, quality gates, etc.)\n   - Go style constitution: copyright header, 40-line function limit, 500-line file limit, write-all-then-build strategy\n   - pkg/testutils provides `BuildBinary`, `RunDiffTests`, `DiffTest` struct, `NormalizeFunc`\n   - pkg/sys provides `InstallSIGPIPEHandler`, `Stat`, `Lstat`, `FileInfo`, `TerminalWidth`, `IsTerminal`\n   - pkg/format provides `HumanSize`, `FileTypeColor`, `PadRight`, `PadLeft`, `Columns`\n\n3. Files and Code Sections:\n   - `docs/specs/software-requirements/srd103-pathchk.yaml` (READ)\n     - Important: Defines the pathchk utility requirements\n     - R1 (Pathname checking): R1.1 (validity on current system), R1.2 (-p POSIX portability: chars A-Za-z0-9._-, component ≤14, path ≤256), R1.3 (-P no leading hyphen), R1.4 (multiple arguments)\n     - R2 (Exit codes/SIGPIPE): R2.1 (exit 0 all pass), R2.2 (exit 1 any fail), R2.3 (SIGPIPE via sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler)\n     - Non-goals: does not create/modify files\n     - Package imports: pkg/sys\n     - Acceptance criteria: AC1 (valid path exits 0), AC2 (-p invalid exits 1), AC3 (diff test passes), AC4 (compiles clean)\n\n   - `docs/specs/test-suites/test-rel-12.1.yaml` (READ)\n     - Important: Defines test cases for pathchk (and tsort, test utilities)\n     - pathchk_valid: args=[\"validpath\"], exit_code=0, traces srd103-pathchk R1.1\n     - pathchk_posix_invalid: args=[\"-p\", \"invalid@path\"], exit_code=1, traces srd103-pathchk R1.2\n     - Preconditions: reference binary gpathchk from Homebrew coreutils\n\n   - `docs/specs/use-cases/rel12.1-uc002-pathchk.yaml` (READ)\n     - Important: Use case describing differential testing flow for pathchk\n     - Actor: Human Operator\n     - Touchpoints: cmd/pathchk, pkg/testutils, pkg/sys\n     - Success: differential test passes for all standard flag combinations\n\n   - All pkg/ source code is provided inline in the prompt (testutils, sys, format, hashutil, sizeparse, encutil) - these are the existing shared libraries to use\n\n4. Errors and fixes:\n   - No errors encountered yet. Only three files were read before the summary was requested.\n\n5. Problem Solving:\n   - Identified pathchk (srd103-pathchk) as the appropriate target for implementation based on the ready requirement states (R2.1, R2.2, R2.3 are marked \"ready\")\n   - Note: Only R2.1-R2.3 appear in requirement_states as \"ready\", but the full SRD also has R1.1-R1.4. The R1 group is not listed in requirement_states at all, which may mean those requirements are in a different state or need to be included for a functional implementation.\n   - Successfully read all three specification files needed to implement pathchk\n\n6. All user messages:\n   - Message 1: The massive cobbler-scaffold task prompt with all project context, requirement_states, execution constitution, go style constitution, and task instructions for generation-run44 task 0 (#5596)\n   - Message 2: \"CRITICAL: Respond with TEXT ONLY. Do NOT call any tools.\" - requesting a detailed summary of the conversation\n\n7. Pending Tasks:\n   - Implement cmd/pathchk/main.go based on srd103-pathchk requirements\n   - Implement cmd/pathchk/pathchk_test.go with differential tests against gpathchk\n   - Verify compilation with `go build -o bin/pathchk ./cmd/pathchk/`\n   - Verify tests pass with `go test ./cmd/pathchk/... -count=1`\n   - Verify `go vet ./cmd/pathchk/...` passes\n\n8. Current Work:\n   I had just completed reading the three specification files for pathchk (SRD, test suite, use case) and was about to begin the implementation phase. I had identified pathchk as the target utility because it has the fewest ready requirements (R2.1-R2.3: exit codes and SIGPIPE handling) among the available SRDs. The full SRD also includes R1.1-R1.4 (pathname checking logic) which would need to be implemented for a functional utility even though they're not listed as \"ready\" in requirement_states.\n\n   The implementation would require creating two files:\n   - `cmd/pathchk/main.go` - Main implementation with flag parsing (-p, -P), pathname validation logic, POSIX portability checks, and SIGPIPE handler\n   - `cmd/pathchk/pathchk_test.go` - Differential tests using `testutils.RunDiffTests` against `gpathchk`\n\n9. Optional Next Step:\n   Write the implementation files for cmd/pathchk based on the SRD requirements just read. The task instructions state: \"**Implement** — Complete the work described in Requirements and Files to Create/Modify. Follow the Design Decisions.\" The SRD (srd103-pathchk) specifies the requirements, and the go_style_constitution says to \"Write all source files before running any build or test commands.\" So the next step would be to write cmd/pathchk/main.go and cmd/pathchk/pathchk_test.go in full, then build and test.\n\nIf you need specific details from before compaction (like exact code snippets, error messages, or content you generated), read the full transcript at: /Users/pdiddy/.claude/projects/-private-var-folders-dl-cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn-T-go-unix-utils-worktrees-5596/96e60321-b696-4fc0-bad2-bb3f752ba841.jsonl\nContinue the conversation from where it left off without asking the user any further questions. Resume directly — do not acknowledge the summary, do not recap what was happening, do not preface with \"I'll continue\" or similar. Pick up the last task as if the break never happened."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"96e60321-b696-4fc0-bad2-bb3f752ba841","uuid":"fe78f3ba-8538-4ce5-a4b0-609725a022bd","timestamp":"2026-05-27T15:21:43.425Z","isSynthetic":true}
