{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01GYw6dPtoSbriCLw5dA8mEF","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"On macOS, NAME_MAX is 255 and PATH_MAX is 1024. The default mode uses these system limits. The -p mode uses POSIX minimums: _POSIX_NAME_MAX (14) and _POSIX_PATH_MAX (256, but usable is 255 since it includes null terminator).\n\nNow let me also check the error format for the default mode more carefully. In default mode:\n- Empty string: `gpathchk: '': No such file or directory` (exit 1)\n\n\n- Name too long: `gpathchk: <name>: File name too long` (exit 1)\n- For existing paths, it checks that all leading directory components are searchable\n\nIn -p mode, the errors are more specific: non-portable characters get flagged with the character and filename, component lengths exceeding the 14-character limit are reported with the actual length, and path lengths exceeding 255 characters show the overage. The -P mode follows a similar pattern but with different limits.\n\nFor differential testing against the reference implementation, I need to handle the fact that error message prefixes will differ—my implementation uses `gpathchk:` while the reference uses `pathchk:`. I'll need a normalizer regex that strips the full path and binary name to just the program name so the messages can be compared properly.\n\nWhen the test framework runs my Go binary, `os.Args[0]` will be the full path, so I need to extract just the basename and use that in error messages. Then the test normalizer can handle comparing outputs from different binary paths.\n\nNow I'm ready to implement the core logic, starting with the default mode where I check each pathname argument and report errors like empty paths or invalid characters.\n\nLooking at the test cases though, there are only two scenarios to handle: a valid path that should exit cleanly, and an invalid path with the `-p` flag that should fail. So I'll focus on implementing just