{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_0134uVsZ4JDvsQT6pwGmsYQB","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"OK, so for control characters, the error message shows the character in `\\NNN` octal format (like `\\001`), and in the file name it uses the bash `$'\\001'` quoting style. Actually wait, looking at the output more carefully:\n\n```\ngpathchk: non-portable character '\\001' in file name 'a'$'\\001''b'\n```\n\nHmm, the quoting of the filename is complex. It uses POSIX shell quoting. The character itself in the \"character\" field is quoted as `\\NNN` in single quotes.\n\n\nFor the differential test, I can run both gpathchk and my implementation against the same inputs and compare their outputs. Since GNU coreutils uses locale-aware quoting functions that produce the `$'\\001'` style in the C locale, I'll need to either match that format exactly or use a normalizer to handle any differences in how we quote filenames in error messages.\n\nLooking at the actual error format, I need to get the quoting right for both printable ASCII characters and control characters like tabs and other non-portable characters. The format varies depending on what character is being quoted, so I'll need to handle those cases carefully.\n\nFor the differential tests, I should focus on simpler cases with printable ASCII characters where the error messages are more straightforward to match, and use normalizers for the more complex quoting scenarios. Since the test suite only has two basic pathchk cases defined, I can add more test cases that stick to simpler error messages to make the matching more manageable. I need to verify the exact quoting format that GNU coreutils uses when displaying filenames in error messages, particularly how it handles different character types in the C locale.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":400,"